+## 5. Measurement boundaries: frozen checked service and fixture workflow
 
-The operation boundary is a CPU, single-threaded, resident-input NTT service. Its immutable fixture is the exact eight dense vectors for one n already present in a caller-owned byte pool. Each useful request independently allocates/materializes its n-entry canonical input from that pool, executes one schedule, returns a newly owned full natural-order output, visits all output entries through the identical observable sink, and destroys its input/output buffers. Input materialization and output lifetime are therefore inside the service timing. No file I/O, text formatting or corpus-generation work is hidden inside one algorithm alone.
+The operation is a CPU, single-threaded, checked resident-input NTT service. All four methods use exactly the same request wrapper. Each request checks supplied element count/size and representability before unsafe access or allocation, independently allocates/materializes its n-entry canonical input from the immutable pool, checks every coefficient is in [0,p-1], executes the schedule, consumes its full natural-order output through the common sink, and destroys the input/output buffers. Ordinary runtime guards, including their scans and failure machinery on the successful path, are inside the paid request lifecycle for useful and warm-up requests alike. The correctness audit separately confirms output length/range, all-coordinate values and input immutability; these audit comparisons are not repeated inside timed requests.
 
